      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "5ce4583e-c99f-532c-8f96-a07269c60d3f",
        ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}^{2}={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}^{2}\\,.\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}^{2} & ={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}^{2} & =1+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}^{2}-2y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & =1-2y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{{2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}_{\u23df}}_{P(x)}\\,y & ={{1}_{\u23df}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ and $Q(x)=1.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "7bbd239e-b8c4-5069-b4e1-e9313244312e",
