        ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{2(\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x)}_{\u23df}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x}_{\u23df}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x$ and $Q(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x.$",
